        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          Luxury vinyl plank and laminate are the two floors most people end up choosing between, and the sales pitch for both has converged: wood look, click-lock install, waterproof or water-resistant, scratch-resistant. Under the printed surface they are very different products. Laminate is a wood-fiber board with a hard mineral-filled top coat. LVP is plastic all the way through. Almost every practical difference follows from that.
        </p>

        <div id="short-answer" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Answer
          </h2>

          <p>
            Choose LVP for any room where water is a regular visitor: kitchens, bathrooms, laundry rooms, mudrooms and basements. Its vinyl core does not absorb water, so a dishwasher leak or a wet dog will not swell the planks. Choose laminate for living rooms, bedrooms, hallways and upper floors, where it resists scratches better, feels more like real wood underfoot and usually costs a little less per square foot.
          </p>

          <p>
            That split is a default, not a law. Better waterproof laminates have narrowed the water gap, and good LVP with a thick wear layer and an attached pad has narrowed the comfort gap. But the defaults hold for most homes, and many households sensibly use both: vinyl in the wet rooms, laminate everywhere else, with a transition strip at the doorway.
          </p>

          <p>
            Three questions settle most rooms. Is there plumbing in the room or a concrete slab under it? Will large dogs or heavy foot traffic be grinding grit into the surface every day? And does it bother you when a floor feels hard or sounds hollow underfoot? Water points to vinyl, claws and comfort point to laminate, and where both apply, water wins, because a swollen laminate floor cannot be repaired and a scratched vinyl one can at least still be lived on.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you have settled on vinyl, our <a href="/best-vinyl-plank-flooring/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best vinyl plank flooring guide</a> ranks 13 floors we soaked, scratched and dented over a season. If laminate looks like the better fit, our <a href="/best-laminate-flooring/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best laminate flooring guide</a> covers the 14 we tested, including the waterproof laminates that now compete directly with LVP. This page is for deciding which list to start with.
          </p>

