        <div id="construction" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. What Each Floor Is Made Of
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate is a layered board. The core is high-density fiberboard, wood fibers compressed with resin. On top sits a printed decor paper, the photograph of wood, and over that a clear melamine wear layer, usually reinforced with aluminum oxide particles. A backing layer underneath balances the board. Planks are typically 7 to 12 millimeters thick and lock together with a milled click joint.
          </p>

          <p>
            Luxury vinyl plank is made almost entirely of PVC. Older flexible LVP is thin and glued down or loose-laid. Most of what is sold today is rigid core vinyl, which comes in two main types. SPC, stone plastic composite, mixes PVC with ground limestone for a dense, stiff, dent-resistant core. WPC, wood plastic composite, uses a foamed PVC core that is thicker, lighter and softer underfoot. Both carry a printed vinyl film and a clear vinyl wear layer, usually with a urethane top coat.
          </p>

          <p>
            The two floors are rated on completely different scales, which is a common source of confusion. Laminate carries an AC, or abrasion class, rating from an abrasion test: AC3 is the standard for all residential rooms, AC4 suits heavy residential and light commercial use, and AC5 is commercial. Vinyl is rated by the thickness of its wear layer in mil, thousandths of an inch: 6 to 8 mil is light-duty, 12 mil is mainstream residential, and 20 mil or more is heavy residential or light commercial.
          </p>

          <p>
            There is no reliable conversion between the two scales, because they measure different materials in different ways. An AC4 laminate is not the same as a 20 mil vinyl. Compare laminates by AC rating and vinyls by wear layer, and for a busy family home look for at least AC3, ideally AC4, in laminate, or at least 12 mil, ideally 20, in vinyl.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="water" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. Water Resistance: The Real Dividing Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            This is the difference that matters most. Water that reaches an untreated fiberboard core makes it swell, usually at the joints first, and the swelling does not go back down when it dries. The edges peak, the planks cup, and the only fix is replacing them. That is why traditional laminate has always been kept out of bathrooms and why a slow dishwasher leak is the classic way laminate kitchens die.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vinyl's core does not absorb water at all. A spill can sit on LVP until you notice it, and a minor leak will not damage the planks themselves. That does not make the floor a sealed tray, though. Water can still run through the seams and around the edges to the subfloor below, where it can feed mold and damage plywood. Waterproof planks protect the floor, not the house, and a major leak still needs the floor lifted and the subfloor dried.
          </p>

          <p>
            Waterproof laminate is a real category, not just marketing, but read the warranty carefully. Products such as Mohawk's RevWood Plus and Pergo's WetProtect lines treat the core and joints to resist water, and that is a large improvement. Pergo's WetProtect warranty, for example, covers everyday spills and wet mopping but excludes flooding, standing water and appliance leaks, and requires the room's perimeter to be sealed with approved quarter round, sealing tape or silicone for full coverage. Vinyl's protection does not depend on how the edges were finished.
          </p>

          <p>
            For basements specifically, vinyl is the safer choice. Concrete slabs can wick moisture up from below, and vinyl tolerates that far better than fiberboard. Test the slab and deal with any dampness first; our guide to <a href="/basement-moisture-control/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">basement moisture control</a> covers the tests and fixes. If the space smells musty or shows damp patches, fix that before laying any floor, vinyl included.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE WATER RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Waterproof planks protect the floor, not the subfloor.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Vinyl will not swell, but water still travels through seams. Laminate rated waterproof resists spills, but most warranties exclude standing water and require sealed edges. In a room with a toilet, dishwasher or washing machine, choose vinyl.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="durability" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Scratches, Dents and Everyday Wear
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate generally wins on scratch resistance. Its melamine and aluminum oxide surface is very hard, and good laminate shrugs off the grit, chair legs and pet claws that leave fine scratches on vinyl. If you have large dogs and the main risk in the room is claws rather than water, a well-rated laminate is often the more durable-looking floor after a few years.
          </p>

          <p>
            Vinyl's resistance depends heavily on its wear layer. A 6 or 8 mil wear layer on budget LVP scratches and shows traffic paths fairly quickly, while 20 mil vinyl holds up very well. In our vinyl testing, a commercial-rated 20 mil SPC floor showed far less wear under a grit abrasion test than mainstream 12 mil products. If you are comparing a cheap LVP to a mid-priced laminate on scratch resistance, the laminate usually wins; a premium LVP closes the gap.
          </p>

          <p>
            Dents are more nuanced. SPC is dense and resists heavy point loads well. WPC is softer and will take permanent impressions from furniture legs, appliance feet and dropped objects unless you use felt pads and wide furniture cups. Laminate resists dents across its surface but can chip at the edges if something heavy is dropped on a joint, and those chips are hard to repair invisibly.
          </p>

          <p>
            Repair is similar for both click-lock floors: damaged planks can be replaced by unclicking rows from the nearest wall, or by cutting out the damaged plank and gluing in a replacement with its locking edge trimmed. Keep a box of spare planks from the same production lot. Color and print vary between lots, and finding a match five years later is unlikely.
          </p>

          <p>
            For cleaning, neither floor likes a soaking. Damp mop rather than wet mop, and avoid steam mops unless the manufacturer specifically approves them; heat and moisture forced into seams can damage both. A robot vacuum is fine on either, and regular grit removal is the single best thing you can do for any wear layer.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="feel" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. Look, Feel and Sound
          </h2>

          <p>
            Both floors are photographs of wood, so the look comes down to print quality, texture and plank variety rather than the material. Better products in either category use embossing registered to the printed grain, so the texture follows the knots and lines you see, and offer enough distinct plank prints that the pattern does not repeat obviously across a room. Cheap versions of both look flat and repetitive.
          </p>

          <p>
            Underfoot, laminate usually feels more like wood. It is thicker and stiffer, and its hard surface has the slight give and solid feel of a wood floor when installed over a good underlayment. Rigid SPC vinyl can feel hard and slightly plasticky, especially over concrete, while WPC is softer and warmer underfoot. Barefoot households often prefer WPC or laminate to SPC.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sound is where laminate earns its main complaint. Thin laminate over a hard subfloor produces a hollow, clicking sound with every step, which immediately reads as fake. Thicker 10 to 12 millimeter laminate over a quality underlayment largely solves it. Vinyl tends to be quieter in the room, especially WPC or any vinyl with an attached cork or foam pad, though SPC can have a sharp tap of its own.
          </p>

          <p>
            Temperature to the touch also differs. Vinyl and laminate both feel warmer than tile, but vinyl over a concrete slab can feel cool in winter. Both work over most hydronic radiant heating systems within the manufacturer's surface temperature limits, so check the installation instructions for the specific product before combining them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="install" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Sun, Temperature, Installation and Underlayment
          </h2>

          <p>
            Vinyl is more sensitive to heat. It expands and contracts with temperature more than laminate does, and strong direct sun through a large south- or west-facing glass door can heat a strip of floor far above room temperature. Installation guides for rigid core vinyl commonly require a temperature-controlled space, warn about prolonged direct sunlight, and exclude sunrooms and unheated seasonal rooms unless the specific product is approved for them. Blinds or UV-filtering window film help.
          </p>

          <p>
            Laminate is more sensitive to humidity than to heat, swelling slightly in damp months and shrinking in dry ones. Both floors are floating floors that need an expansion gap at every wall and fixed object, typically around a quarter of an inch, hidden under baseboard or quarter round. Pinning a floating floor with trim nailed into it, or running it tight under cabinets, is the most common cause of buckling for either type.
          </p>

          <p>
            Installation is similar and very DIY-friendly. Both click together over a flat, clean subfloor. Rigid vinyl can usually be cut with a utility knife by scoring and snapping, which is quieter and cleaner than the saw laminate needs. Laminate is less forgiving of an uneven subfloor and usually needs a tighter flatness tolerance. When you remove and reinstall baseboards, nail them into the wall studs, not the floor; our sibling guide on <a href="/how-to-find-a-stud-without-a-stud-finder/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">finding a stud without a stud finder</a> helps if your trim nails do not line up with the old holes.
          </p>

          <p>
            Underlayment rules differ. Laminate almost always needs a foam or cork underlayment unless one is attached, plus a vapor barrier over concrete. Many rigid vinyl planks now come with an attached pad, and adding a second layer under them can flex the locking joints and void the warranty. Our guide to the <a href="/best-underlayment-for-vinyl-plank-flooring/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">best underlayment for vinyl plank flooring</a> explains when vinyl needs one and when it must not have one.
          </p>

          <p>
            Acclimation is worth respecting for both. Most manufacturers ask for the boxes to sit in the room for a period before installation, often around 48 hours, at normal living temperature and humidity. Skipping it is a common cause of gaps and peaking in the first season.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            7. Cost, Resale and Room-by-Room Verdict
          </h2>

          <p>
            Laminate is usually the cheaper floor. Standard laminate runs roughly $1 to $3 per square foot for material, with premium waterproof lines above that. Rigid core vinyl typically runs about $2 to $6 per square foot, more for thick wear layers and premium WPC. Professional installation adds roughly $1.50 to $5 per square foot for either, depending on the region and the amount of prep and trim work, so installed prices overlap heavily.
          </p>

          <p>
            Neither adds value to a home the way solid hardwood can, but both read as an upgrade over worn carpet, and buyers increasingly expect hard floors in kitchens and living areas. A good-quality floor in either material that is in clean condition helps a sale; a cheap floor that is peaked at the dishwasher or scratched through in the hallway hurts it. Match the floor to the room and it will still look good when you sell.
          </p>

          <p>
            Kitchens, bathrooms, laundry rooms, mudrooms and basements: LVP, ideally SPC with at least a 12 mil wear layer, and 20 mil if the room takes heavy traffic or pets. Living rooms, bedrooms, dens and upstairs hallways: laminate, AC3 at minimum and AC4 if the room sees heavy use, in a 10 to 12 millimeter plank if hollow sound bothers you.
          </p>

          <p>
            Homes with large dogs face a trade-off between claws and accidents. Laminate resists scratches better, vinyl resists accidents better. For most pet owners vinyl with a 20 mil wear layer is the safer all-round choice. For an open-plan ground floor where the kitchen flows into the living room and you want one continuous floor, choose vinyl, because the kitchen's water risk sets the standard for the whole space.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE DECISION RULE</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Water decides first. Then scratches, feel and price.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Any room with plumbing or a concrete slab: vinyl. Dry rooms where scratches, wood-like feel and cost matter most: laminate. One continuous floor through a kitchen: vinyl.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- Common Mistakes -->
        <div id="mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            8. Common Mistakes to Avoid
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6 my-8">
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Putting standard laminate in a bathroom</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                A fiberboard core that absorbs water swells permanently, and bathrooms deliver water from tubs, toilets and wet feet every day. Even waterproof laminate usually requires a sealed perimeter, and its warranty often excludes standing water. In bathrooms and laundry rooms, vinyl is the floor that forgives a slow leak.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Comparing AC ratings to mil thickness</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Laminate's AC rating and vinyl's wear layer measure different things on different materials, and there is no reliable conversion. A salesperson who says an AC4 equals a 20 mil vinyl is guessing. Compare laminates by AC rating, vinyls by wear layer, and both by warranty terms for your intended room.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Adding underlayment under padded vinyl</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Many rigid vinyl planks ship with a pad already attached. Laying a second foam underlayment beneath them lets the joints flex, which can break the locking edges and often voids the warranty. Check whether your planks have an attached pad before buying underlayment, and use only what the manufacturer specifies.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Pinning a floating floor</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Both floors expand and contract and need a gap at every wall and fixed object. Nailing baseboard or quarter round down into the planks, or butting the floor tight against cabinets, stops that movement and causes buckling. Nail trim into the wall, leave the gap, and use flexible transitions at doorways.
              </p>
            </div>
            <div class="bg-red-50 border border-red-100 rounded-2xl p-6">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-red-900 mt-0 mb-2">Buying cheap vinyl for pets</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm m-0">
                Budget LVP with a 6 or 8 mil wear layer scratches quickly under dog claws, and vinyl scratches cannot be sanded out. If pets are the reason you are choosing vinyl, pay for a 20 mil wear layer, or consider a good AC4 laminate in dry rooms where accidents are less of a concern.
              </p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <!-- FAQ -->
        <div id="faq" class="scroll-mt-24 mt-16">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mb-6">
            9. Frequently Asked Questions
          </h2>

          <div class="space-y-6">
            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is LVP better than laminate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                LVP is better in wet areas because its vinyl core does not absorb water, so it suits kitchens, bathrooms and basements. Laminate is usually better in dry rooms, where it resists scratches better, feels more like wood and costs a little less. Neither is better everywhere, and many homes sensibly use vinyl in wet rooms and laminate elsewhere.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which is more waterproof, LVP or laminate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                LVP. Its core is plastic and does not swell, so spills and minor leaks do not damage the planks. Waterproof laminates resist topical spills far better than older laminate, but their warranties typically exclude standing water and flooding and require sealed edges. With either floor, water can still pass through seams and damage the subfloor.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Which scratches more easily, vinyl or laminate?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Vinyl usually scratches more easily, because laminate's melamine and aluminum oxide surface is harder. The gap depends on the wear layer: budget 6 to 8 mil vinyl scratches quickly, while 20 mil vinyl holds up well. For homes with large dogs and dry rooms, a good AC4 laminate often stays looking newer for longer.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Is laminate or vinyl better for a kitchen?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Vinyl, in most kitchens. Dishwashers, sinks and refrigerators with ice makers are the classic sources of slow leaks, and a fiberboard core swells permanently when water reaches it. If you prefer laminate's look and feel, choose a waterproof laminate, follow its perimeter sealing instructions exactly and read what its warranty excludes.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does LVP or laminate cost more?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                LVP usually costs a little more for material, roughly $2 to $6 per square foot for rigid core against about $1 to $3 for standard laminate, with premium products in both categories above those ranges. Professional installation costs are similar for both, so installed prices overlap heavily. Thick wear layers and waterproof laminates push each toward the top.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Can you put LVP or laminate in a basement?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Vinyl is the better basement floor, because concrete slabs can release moisture that would swell a fiberboard core. Test the slab for moisture first and fix any dampness before installing anything. Use a vapor barrier where the manufacturer requires one, and keep a perimeter expansion gap. Standard laminate is generally a poor basement choice.
              </p>
            </div>

            <div class="bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
              <h3 class="text-lg font-bold text-gray-900 mb-2">Does laminate or vinyl sound more hollow?</h3>
              <p class="text-gray-700 text-sm leading-relaxed">
                Laminate is more likely to sound hollow, especially thin planks over a hard subfloor. Thicker 10 to 12 millimeter laminate over a good underlayment greatly reduces it. Vinyl is usually quieter in the room, particularly WPC or planks with an attached cork or foam pad, though rigid SPC can still produce a sharp tap underfoot.
              </p>
            </div>

          </div>
        </div>

